<div class="meta">Lesson 0019 · Rendering · from issue #67 (atmosphere pass)</div>
<h1>Distance Fog in a Forward Renderer</h1>
<p>Fog is the cheapest atmosphere money can buy: one shader, two uniforms,
and the hard horizon line disappears. The interesting parts are the blend
math and making sure <em>every</em> draw path goes through it.</p>

<h2>The math: depth-based color blending</h2>
<pre><code>d   = length(cameraPos - fragPosition) * fogDensity
fog = 1.0 - exp(-d * d)                 // squared exponential
finalColor.rgb = mix(litColor, fogColor, fog)</code></pre>
<table>
<tr><th>Curve</th><th>Character</th></tr>
<tr><td>linear (start/end)</td><td>Hard band; obvious where fog "begins"</td></tr>
<tr><td>exp</td><td>Fades immediately — even nearby objects dull</td></tr>
<tr><td>exp² (used here)</td><td>Flat near the camera, then rolls off fast —
plaza crisp at 40 units (~5%), far corner ~80% at 220</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Two choices carry the whole look: <strong>fogColor = the sky clear
color</strong>, so geometry melts into the horizon instead of graying out,
and a <strong>warm multiply</strong> (1.06, 1.00, 0.92) applied <em>before</em>
fogging so distance still cools toward the sky.</p>

<h2>Every draw path must fog, or the seams show</h2>
<p>raylib has two distinct 3D paths, each with its own shader binding:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meshes</strong> (buildings, roads, cars, characters):
<code>DrawMesh</code> uses <code>material.shader</code> — assign the fog
shader to every material at load.</li>
<li><strong>Batch primitives</strong> (grass plane, slabs, the fountain,
the viewmodel): drawn through rlgl's immediate batch, which uses the shader
set by <code>BeginShaderMode</code>. Miss this and the buildings haze while
the grass stays neon-green to the horizon.</li>
</ul>
<p>One shader serves both because the vertex stage computes
<code>fragPosition = matModel * vertexPosition</code> — and batch vertices
arrive already in world space with <code>matModel = identity</code>, so the
same expression is correct in both paths.</p>

<h2>Skinning survives — and why</h2>
<p>raylib 5.5 skins characters on the CPU: <code>UpdateModelAnimation</code>
writes posed vertices into the mesh before drawing. The fog shader consumes
only standard attributes (position/texcoord/color), so animation is
untouched. A GPU-skinned pipeline would <em>not</em> be this forgiving — the
custom vertex shader would need the bone matrices.</p>

<h2>Ownership trap: shared shaders vs UnloadModel</h2>
<div class="callout warn"><code>UnloadModel</code> deliberately does NOT
free material shaders/textures — raylib's comment says users may share them
across models. That contract is what makes one-shader-many-materials safe,
but it also means <em>you</em> must unload the shared shader exactly once
(Assets' destructor), and standalone <code>UnloadMaterial</code> WOULD free
it — never call that on materials holding a shared shader.</div>

<div class="callout good"><strong>Budget honesty.</strong> The issue budget
was "one shader, two uniforms" — no shadow maps, no post-processing, no
day/night. Fog + tint delivered the atmosphere for one uniform vector, one
float, and a per-frame camera position.</div>
